From d56e5c0d58df3b9abb900ad8a62e6b25d67a6186 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:31:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/20] relay_metrics: Count HSDir fetches per descriptor and
 minute

Whether a flood fetches a few onion service descriptors again and again,
sweeps many of them, or asks for keys that miss calls for different
fixes: a limit per descriptor, a limit per circuit or source, or a limit
on misses. HSDirDescriptorFetchRate's count covers only descriptors in
the cache, and only at its own rate.

Count each fetch that reaches the cache, by whether it found the
descriptor (lookup "found" or "not_found", as
tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_total says, except that "found" also counts the
fetches HSDirFetchShedding then refuses) and by its place among the
fetches of the same blinded key in the same minute, in the bands the
source counts use, as tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_descriptor_total. Decode the
key right after the lookup, for misses too, so that every spelling of a
key in the URL counts as that key (base64 decoding stops at the first
"="), and count a key that doesn't decode as a first fetch. Keep only a
keyed hash of each key (siphash24g, whose key is random at each start)
with its count, which stops at the last band, in a 64 KB table that the
first fetch of each new minute clears. A key that finds no slot within
16 of its own counts as a first fetch; a few do once a minute has more
than about 2,000 distinct keys, so in a minute that sweeps keys, or
whose fetches of random keys fill the table on purpose, the later bands
undercount repeats. Nothing per key is exported. Bridges don't count
these, nor does a relay without a MetricsPort, the only place they are
shown. Only dircache.c, which is built only with the relay module, calls
the counter, so it needs no stub.

Part of #41339.
